{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Abram: Go you, etc.</b> What would the verse have lacked had it said only 'Go you to the land that I will show you,' and why did it specify these details — 'from your land and from your birthplace and from your father's house'? Also the word 'לְךָ' needs explanation, for its meaning cannot be 'for your own benefit.' And the explanation given by the Ramban, that it is in the language of 'אֵלֵךְ לִי אֶל הַר הַמּוֹר' (Song of Songs 4:6), does not seem right, for there too 'לִי' no less than 'לְךָ' would require some other explanation, the one closer to the plain sense being preferable. And it appears to me that the Holy One, blessed be He, transferred him from subject to subject in the manner of training a child, for perhaps it would be too hard upon him to accept everything at once. Therefore He made for him three divisions and transferred him to a fourth subject. For first He asked him to leave his land, and this is a small demand, since a person derives no greater benefit from his own land than from other lands — only the name and imagined honor that people accord him in a place where they recognize him. And besides this, he has no help or support from all the distant people of his land, and he would easily be persuaded to abandon them. And afterward, once he had removed all thought of his land from his heart, He said: perhaps his soul is bound to the people of his birthplace, from whom he has some help and support, in the manner of families where each one enters the thickness of the beam for the sake of his kin, and they give no room to strangers to rule over them. And this is somewhat more of a benefit than the first. And after he was persuaded of this too, He worried lest his soul cleave to his father's house, where he has a benefit beyond all these, namely the benefit of wealth, for 'house and wealth are the inheritance of fathers' (Proverbs 19:14)." "In addition to these three kinds of closeness, there is a fourth closeness: for a person is close to himself more than to all three kinds of relatives. Therefore it is said 'לֶךְ לְךָ' — to your very self, in the manner spoken to man that he should go to work the ground from which he was taken. And above, in parashat Bereishit (Genesis 3:23), I explained that this refers to Mount Moriah, for man was created from the place of his atonement, and his source is Mount Moriah, for from there his dust was taken. And the pathway of the ladder in the Temple below is aligned opposite the Temple above, upon which the pure souls ascend and descend. If so, there is the selfhood of the body, and there is the hidden potency of the soul. And if so, a person is closest to his own selfhood more than to any of the kinds of relatives mentioned, therefore 'לֶךְ לְךָ' is said. And so too this expression is used at the Binding (Genesis 22:2): 'וְלֶךְ לְךָ אֶל אֶרֶץ הַמֹּרִיָּה,' etc. And in both of them He did not reveal the place immediately, for here it says 'אֲשֶׁר אַרְאֶךָּ,' and at the Binding it says 'אֲשֶׁר אֹמַר אֵלֶיךָ.' Because the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to reveal to Abram the place of origin of body and soul until His holy spirit would first be poured upon him from on high. For it is known (Tanhuma Bo 5) that prophecy does not rest outside the land. And if so, all the while that he was outside the land, before the spirit of prophecy was upon him, he would neither know nor understand the nature of the soul, whose source is Mount Moriah, nor even the nature of the formation of the matter from there would he understand, until he came into the Holy Land, the place prepared for prophecy. Then HaShem, blessed be He, showed him the qualities of that holy place, and how it is the place of man's very selfhood — for there is the home and quarry of the soul, and the hewing-place of the matter, for whose sake it was fitting for him to leave all these and go to cleave to that holy place, for there he would attain cleaving to the Shekhinah. And this is what Rashi explained: 'go and journey southward' — to go to the south of Jerusalem and Mount Moriah." "<b>And for this reason, at the beginning of this vision it does not say 'and YHWH appeared to him.'</b> For that was only after he had entered the land, for then he was prepared to see in visions of the Shekhinah. But before this, while he was still outside the land, he heard only a voice of words, for YHWH had not yet appeared to him until he came to the land, as was explained above. And therefore he called that place 'סוּלָמָא דְּצוֹר,' as is found in the Yalkut on this parashah (Yalkut Shimoni 12a): 'When Abraham reached סוּלָמָא דְּצוֹר, he said: May my portion be in this land.' Who told the author of the midrash that Abraham reached סוּלָמָא דְּצוֹר? Surely his intent is upon Mount Moriah, for there is the place of the ladder that Jacob saw standing in Beit El, and by way of that ladder the souls ascend and descend, and there is the Rock of his hewing for the body, as it is written (Isaiah 51:1): 'Look to the rock from which you were hewn.' For from that rock, which is called the Even Shetiyah, the world at large was founded, and also man, who is called a microcosm, was fashioned from the place of that rock, in which is the place of the ladder also for the soul, as has been said. And for this reason 'לֶךְ לְךָ' is said here, and at the Binding, and likewise in the verse 'אֵלֵךְ לִי אֶל הַר הַמּוֹר,' and likewise 'the rain is over and gone' (Song of Songs 2:11) — it went to the place of its source, for 'a mist went up from the earth' (Genesis 2:6). And likewise (Deuteronomy 2:13): 'Arise and cross for yourselves the Nahal Zered' — this too means to the land of Israel. And likewise (Jeremiah 5:5): 'I will go for myself to the great ones' — for there is the dwelling of the whole ones. And likewise every 'לִי' and 'לוֹ' and 'לְךָ' and 'לָהֶם' in Scripture he explained in this manner. And Abraham understood from the word 'לֶךְ לְךָ' that he was to go to the land of Canaan, even though it was not stated to which land he would go."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:09.447012615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}